@startuml DashboardTerminalDesign
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
skinparam noteTextAlignment left
' purpose: how the proveo dashboard spends terminal cells — item density borrowed from sbx, width and height breakpoints, and a readable change tree
' covers: internal/dashboard/layout.go
' covers: internal/dashboard/items.go
' covers: internal/dashboard/filetree.go
' covers: internal/dashboard/view.go
' covers: internal/dashboard/model.go
' covers: internal/dashboard/layout_test.go
' covers: internal/dashboard/filetree_test.go
' covers: e2e/dashboard_breakpoints_tmux_test.go
' covers: internal/dashboard/popover.go
' covers: internal/dashboard/popover_test.go
' covers: internal/dashboard/cards.go
' covers: internal/dashboard/highlight.go
' covers: internal/dashboard/highlight_test.go
' covers: internal/dashboard/handoff.go
' covers: internal/dashboard/handoff_test.go
' covers: internal/dashboard/ports.go
' covers: internal/dashboard/ports_test.go
'   the plan this upgrades ............ _spec/_plans/dashboard-delivery.puml (D6)
'   the runtime it renders ............ _spec/internal/dashboard/workspace-instances.puml
'   the chosen layout ................ 1 Review queue, lower-right split (HTML prototype retired 2026-11-28)
'   the design language it speaks ..... _spec/_conventions/tui-design-language.puml (glyph tiers, sbx's OWN TUI)
'   the folder picker it does not reuse _spec/internal/choiceui/scope-tree.puml
' Notes are capped at 5 lines x 50 columns; one idea per anchor.
' status: IMPLEMENTED 2026-11-24. Live sbx parity stays with D7.
'
' ── DECIDED 2026-11-25 (the Executor) ───────────────────────────────────────
' "The task details should always be a popover approach, no scroll": t opens a
' rounded card over the task pane, under the selected task or above it when
' the rows below run out. It is sized to its content: title and status, meta,
' sandbox, usage are kept; branch and PR, changes, push, ticket, excerpt and at
' most three prompt lines drop from the bottom to fit. It never scrolls. This
' supersedes the lower-right split, the accordion and the left pane, and the 4
' key that cycled them. Esc closes the card first.
' "proveo's scroll should only be the task list panes": arrows and the wheel move
' through tasks; f or g moves focus to the file tree, whose footer leads with
' Esc back to tasks; Tab swaps tasks and tree. Only an open file preview
' scrolls besides them. JUDGEMENT: a preview longer than its pane still needs
' its own scroll; ask before turning it into a card.
' "diff AND code highlighting must be attached": the preview is read-only
' review (internal/dashboard/highlight.go). One sbx exec reads both sides in
' full, the file at the merge base (its old name after a rename) and the
' sandbox's copy, each capped at 128 KB; carried tasks read the merge base
' and the tip. chroma v2 colours each whole file, so a line inside /* */
' keeps the comment tone a patch hunk would lose; go-udiff lines the two up
' with three lines of context. "Let the code highlighting go beyond proveo's
' color palette; the only exception": code takes a chroma style, default
' catppuccin-mocha, PROVEO_CODE_THEME names another; foreground only, so the
' pane keeps its background; +/- gutters stay identity green and red.
' Keys while a file has focus: n next file, p previous file, d file or diff
' ("`n` should become next file perhaps `p` previous file, `d` toggle diff"),
' u unified, i side by side; [ ] stay as aliases and step from the tree too.
' n means new instance everywhere else. Steps follow tree order, up is
' previous and down is next, opening folders on the way and keeping the mode.
' The title
' names the mode and "7 of 24". The left tree stays review-only: "I want the
' left pane file-tree to be review only purposes". Fresh (e) is the one
' editing path and hangs off the task, not the tree.
' Chosen 2026-11-25 over Fresh hand-off and delta: "chroma, both sides read",
' toggle key. Licences: chroma MIT, go-udiff BSD-3; Fresh GPL-3.0 stays a
' guest tool; Crush diffview FSL-1.1 not copied.
'   https://github.com/alecthomas/chroma
'   https://github.com/aymanbagabas/go-udiff
'   https://github.com/jesseduffield/lazygit/blob/master/docs/Custom_DiffRenderers.md
'   https://getfresh.dev/docs/features/git
' "Add a loading indicator when attach-detaching around from tasks/sbx items"
' (internal/dashboard/handoff.go): attach and Fresh hand the terminal over
' behind a card on the alternate screen ("Attaching to <task>", the sandbox,
' Ctrl+B d detaches back here). The program draws over it; tmux's detach
' restores the shell's own screen, and the dashboard returns with "Back from
' <task>" in the footer. In the sbx tab a frame blank for 250 ms gets "Starting
' sbx" (first start) or "Waiting for sbx" (between its list and an agent) with
' a spinner on the repaint tick. proveo draws only over an empty frame, never
' over sbx's own output. MEASURED: sbx drew its list within 0.5 s here.
' "show a loading card for the file-tree left pane when loading/buffering":
' a read the operator caused (task change, f, g, a new PR base) shows a
' spinner card after 150 ms when the pane is empty ("Reading changes · 23s",
' from the sandbox, "sandbox busy · CPU 13.9/14 cores") and a header spinner
' over rows it refreshes; the 5 s poll refreshes quietly and never restarts a
' read still running. MEASURED 2026-11-25: 1b3fcb7f ran a typecheck and next
' dev at load 109; git merge-base took 13 s and the diff 17 s, so the old 6 s
' guest timeout killed every read and the pane said "No changes". Tree and
' preview reads now get a minute; a failed read says why in the pane, not the
' footer, since the footer's keys matter more.
' "the running task cards must have `p` for the bound `sbx` ports. Display in
' a pop-up" (2026-11-26): p on a card whose sandbox runs opens a notice card of
' sbx ls's published ports: http://host:port → sandbox port/proto, "listening"
' when a host-side dial answers in 300 ms, and the devports tool and file that
' serves that sandbox port. It opens at once with "checking…" while the probe
' and the repo scan run. Card actions fit their width: two spaces, then f files
' drops first. MEASURED: sbx ls --json gives host_ip, host_port, sandbox_port,
' protocol (tcp4); a codex sandbox listed ports null.
' "Make the pane selection more explicit; a cyan border on the left/right panes
' that highlight wherever the user is currently focused at. Let tab switch
' between panes" (2026-11-26): each pane is a rounded frame whose top edge
' carries its title; the focused pane's frame is brand (cyan), the other the
' rule grey. Tab moves between the tree and the right pane (the tasks, or the
' open file); in a single pane it swaps which one shows. The old │ divider
' column is now the one-cell gap between the frames, so it still lines up with
' sbx's divider. A frame costs one row and four columns; tree messages wrap.
' "The file i'm currently reading in the file-tree must be highlighted ... add an
' underline along it on the same cyan color. tab + n should resolve in opening
' the first file and highlighting it": the file being read keeps the › marker
' and a bold name underlined in brand cyan whichever pane has focus. n and p
' from the tree open the file under the cursor, else the first after it, then
' step; n stays new instance on the task cards.


' "the file-tree is still showing the host's files": Files and Changes now read
' the selected task's sandbox (internal/dashboard/sandbox_reads.go): the running
' guest over sbx exec, else the carried tip, else the base; never the host
' checkout. The tree's summary names its source: sandbox, carried <sha> or base.
' "The item list having only 2 rows seems too bland. I'd like a presentation
' more akin to a card; similar to the way sbx does it": each task is a rounded
' card (internal/dashboard/cards.go): the phase as a chip on the top edge, the
' status glyph and title, a divider, agent · ticket · every PR (#3975 #3974),
' then "ticket 3d - PR 7h" (Linear createdAt, the oldest PR's createdAt; the
' task's age when neither is known), then the resources layer: the sandbox and
' its last sample on two surface-tone rows, then the keys, lit on the selected
' card only. "In the task items, give the resources its' own layers, and the
' <ticket age> - <pr age> in a separate row." Cards are 10 rows. Cards
' flow 1 to 3 abreast by width (38-cell minimum); up and down take the nearest
' card in the next row, left and right step the order; + n new instance ends
' the Queued section as an empty card in its last row's free slot, as sbx ends
' with + New Sandbox. "the new instance card must always show under the Queued
' section" (2026-11-27): with nothing queued the Queued heading still shows,
' holding only that card; the Schedule view's heading there reads Waiting for
' dispatch. Before, it joined whichever section came last. A short terminal
' draws five-row cards. This supersedes the two-row item.
' Changes read as the PR will: against the branch's base (vs origin/dev), live
' from the guest when it runs; see workspace-instances.puml (base).
' "The separation of the host resources at the top and the content panels
' isn't clear enough; add a gray muted square around it (similar to sbx
' header)": host capacity sits in a muted rounded frame, three rows, as sbx
' frames its title bar; short frames still drop it.
'
' ── MEASURED 2026-11-24 (HEAD 8f276ba2, tmux 3.7b, six seeded tasks) ────────
' sbx card ....... 37x11 cells, about 12 fields: status dot, name, kind badge,
'                  agent, path, CPU% and cores, RAM, disk, age, state, and the
'                  stop / exec / remove row with a highlighted mnemonic
' HTML reference . 2 rows per task, 5 fields: title, ticket, agent, phase and a
'                  right-aligned status
' HEAD row ....... 1 row, 2 fields (title, phase); about 33 of 84 columns used
' HEAD tree ...... 18 changes took 33 rows: every folder open, one cyan for
'                  A/M/R, a raw "R100" score, no filter and no icons
' HEAD sizes ..... one fixed 30/70 split from 40x12 to 200x50; at 40x12 every
'                  file name read "M …" and the footer cut "Ctrl+C qu…"
' HEAD footer .... a schedule.yml validation error kept only its path at 120
'                  columns; the cause sat past the right edge
'
' ── READING A TMUX PANE (e2e/dashboard_breakpoints_tmux_test.go) ─────────────
' capture-pane prints a TAB where the renderer moved the cursor with HT over
' blank cells; expand it to the next 8-column stop, clamped to the last column,
' or a full-width row measures one cell wider than the pane.
' Right after resize-window the pane still holds the previous size's frame, so
' each size polls until its own layout predicate holds instead of sleeping.
' Escape, Escape, "-" sent in one burst parse as Alt+"-"; send them apart.
' Nerd codepoints: lazygit's MIT table, pkg/gui/presentation/icons/file_icons.go.
'
' ── SIZES THE TIERS ARE CUT FROM ─────────────────────────────────────────────
' 80x24 ......... VT100 heritage; GNOME presets 80x24 80x43 132x24 132x43
'                 https://help.gnome.org/gnome-terminal/app-terminal-sizes.html
' 80x25 ......... legacy Windows console, 0x190050
'                 https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/windows/it-pro/windows-server-2003/cc738363(v=ws.10)
' 120x30 ........ Windows Terminal initialCols / initialRows
'                 https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/terminal/customize-settings/startup
' <=84 x >=46 ... lazygit portraitModeAutoMaxWidth / MinHeight
'                 https://github.com/jesseduffield/lazygit/blob/master/docs/Config.md
' >=200 ......... lazygit: two 80-column main panels plus a 40-column side
'                 https://github.com/jesseduffield/lazygit/blob/master/pkg/gui/controllers/helpers/window_arrangement_helper.go
' 0/80/120 ...... Textual HORIZONTAL_BREAKPOINTS docstring example
'                 https://textual.textualize.io/api/app/#textual.app.App.HORIZONTAL_BREAKPOINTS
' 0/60/90/120/160 ftui-layout default tiers
'                 https://docs.rs/ftui-layout/0.4.1/ftui_layout/struct.Breakpoints.html
' too small ..... bpytop's too-small screen swallowed q, so exit stays live
'                 https://lightrun.com/answers/aristocratos-bpytop-not-possible-to-exit-bpytop-in-a-small-terminal
' short panes ... VS Code panel users report about five visible lines
'                 https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code/issues/26631
'
' ── TREE PRIOR ART ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
' Fresh: compact chains on by default, status priority U>D>A>M>R>?, icons opt-in
'   https://github.com/sinelaw/fresh/blob/master/docs/features/file-explorer.md
'   https://github.com/sinelaw/fresh/blob/master/crates/fresh-editor/plugins/git_explorer.ts
'   https://github.com/sinelaw/fresh/issues/2032 (font detection is infeasible)
'   https://github.com/sinelaw/fresh/blob/master/crates/fresh-editor/tests/e2e/file_explorer_compact_chain.rs
' lazygit: Compress(), ` tree/flat, - collapse all, = expand all
'   https://github.com/jesseduffield/lazygit/blob/master/pkg/gui/filetree/build_tree.go
' broot: a pattern filters the tree and keeps its shape
'   https://github.com/Canop/broot/blob/main/website/src/navigation.md
' Nerd codepoints: lazygit's MIT table
'   https://github.com/jesseduffield/lazygit/blob/master/pkg/gui/presentation/icons/file_icons.go
' fzf promises no library API; sahilm/fuzzy would add a module
'   https://github.com/junegunn/fzf/issues/1010
' Survey of the candidates (own model, bubbles tree, lipgloss tree, treeview,
' teacup): research run 2026-11-24, recorded in the session that wrote this.
title Dashboard terminal design — sbx-dense items, size tiers, a readable change tree

package "ITEM — what one task carries" as ITEMS {
  component "sbx card\n37x11 · about 12 fields" as SBXCARD <<cloud>>
  component "HEAD row\n1 row · title + phase" as OLDROW <<error>>
  component "task card\nphase chip · ● title · status\nagent · ticket · #PRs\nticket age - PR age\nresources band · keys" as ITEM <<app>>
  component "group rule\nlabel ───────" as GROUP <<app>>
  component "n new instance\nfull-width outline" as NEWBTN <<app>>
}

package "TIERS — cut by cells, decided per frame" as TIERS {
  component "too small\n< 40x10\ncard + Ctrl+C" as TINY <<error>>
  component "single\n< 60 cols\none pane, Tab swaps" as SINGLE <<app>>
  component "portrait\n<= 84 cols, >= 46 rows\nChanges above queue" as PORTRAIT <<app>>
  component "split\n>= 60 cols\nsbx-matched divider" as SPLIT <<host>>
  component "short\n< 16 rows\nno capacity frame" as SHORT <<async>>
  component "footer\nquit first · status tail\nkept over hints" as FOOT <<app>>
}

package "TREE — the Changes and Files pane" as TREE {
  component "compact chains\ninternal/backend/sandbox" as COMPACT <<app>>
  component "fold default\nopen when it fits\nelse top level only" as FOLD <<app>>
  component "/ filter\nterms match the name\nor a folder segment" as FILTER <<app>>
  component "status colour\nA green · M lime\nD red · R cyan" as COLOUR <<app>>
  component "folded roll-up\n+3 ~1 -1" as ROLLUP <<app>>
  component "PROVEO_GLYPHS\nnerd icons · safe ▸▾\nascii +- · none" as GLYPH <<host>>
}

SBXCARD ARROW_OPTIONAL ITEM : borrow fields\nand the frame
OLDROW ARROW_ERROR ITEM : replaced
ITEM ARROW_MAIN GROUP
GROUP ARROW_MAIN NEWBTN
TINY ARROW_MAIN SINGLE : widen
SINGLE ARROW_MAIN SPLIT : 60 cols
SPLIT ARROW_OPTIONAL PORTRAIT : narrow and tall
SPLIT ARROW_OPTIONAL SHORT : under 16 rows
COMPACT ARROW_MAIN FOLD
FOLD ARROW_MAIN FILTER
FILTER ARROW_MAIN COLOUR
COLOUR ARROW_MAIN ROLLUP
GLYPH ARROW_OPTIONAL COLOUR : marks only

note bottom of ITEM
  **A task is a card, in sbx's grammar.**
  The phase rides the top edge, status leads the
  title, and the keys light only when selected.
  Cards sit two or three abreast as width allows.
end note

note right of OLDROW
  **One row held two fields and little else.**
  A reviewer could not tell a finished run from a
  failed one without opening the details first.
end note

note bottom of GROUP
  **Recency orders a group, not the job name.**
  The newest outcome leads Attention required, so
  overnight work reads in the order it landed.
end note

note bottom of TINY
  **Below forty by ten nothing fits, so say so.**
  The card names the size and the minimum, and
  Ctrl+C still quits, unlike bpytop's lock-up.
end note

note bottom of SINGLE
  **A narrow split starves both panes at once.**
  Under sixty columns one pane fills the width and
  Tab swaps it, so a file name stays readable.
end note

note bottom of PORTRAIT
  **A tall narrow terminal has rows to spare.**
  Inside lazygit's portrait bounds the tree stacks
  over the queue instead of squeezing beside it.
end note

note bottom of SPLIT
  **From sixty columns the divider follows sbx.**
  Matching the stock column keeps the tab switch
  steady, which the existing tmux checks assert.
end note

note bottom of SHORT
  **A VS Code panel can show only a few lines.**
  Short frames drop the capacity row, and details
  replace the queue rather than halving it.
end note

note bottom of FOOT
  **The status tail outranks low-priority keys.**
  An error tends to start with a path and end with
  its cause, so hints drop before the tail does.
end note

note bottom of COMPACT
  **A chain of lone folders costs a single row.**
  The row keys on the deepest path, so the cursor
  survives when a rebuild absorbs its folder.
end note

note bottom of FOLD
  **Open everything only when everything fits.**
  A carried change set usually fits and reads
  open; a workspace walk does not and starts shut.
end note

note bottom of FILTER
  **The filter keeps the tree shape around hits.**
  A term matches a file name as a subsequence or a
  folder segment as a substring, to limit noise.
end note

note bottom of COLOUR
  **The letter and the name share one colour.**
  Folders stay in ink and a compacted parent dims,
  so colour marks change rather than structure.
end note

note bottom of ROLLUP
  **A folded folder still reports what it hides.**
  A narrow row shortens the name, not the counts,
  so a delete inside cannot vanish off the edge.
end note

note bottom of GLYPH
  **No terminal reports its font, so ask for it.**
  Nerd codepoints draw only under the declared
  tier; every other tier stays standard Unicode.
end note
@enduml
